1
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:22,600
Every iOS app you've ever used — the one that books your rides, the one that plays your
music — comes down to the same handful of foundations. A language, a set of tools, and a way
of connecting what's on screen to code that runs. That's this module.

2
00:00:22,600 --> 00:00:28,600
Here is what you will be able to do by the end of this video.

3
00:00:28,600 --> 00:00:38,200
Let's start with what iOS development is. It means building for Apple's platforms — iPhone,
iPad, Apple Watch, Apple TV. The language is Swift.

4
00:00:38,200 --> 00:00:55,800
A little history helps here. Before Swift, Apple development was done in Objective-C, and
you'll still meet that in older codebases. Swift was announced on the second of June, twenty
fourteen. Then in twenty nineteen came SwiftUI, a newer, declarative way of building

5
00:00:55,800 --> 00:01:17,800
This course teaches Swift with UIKit, not SwiftUI, and here is why. UIKit is what the
overwhelming majority of existing production code is written in. And doing layout by hand in
UIKit first is what makes SwiftUI's shortcuts legible instead of magical. You'll understand

6
00:01:17,800 --> 00:01:27,400
To build anything you need a Mac, Xcode, and a free Apple account. Xcode is the whole
toolkit — editor, interface builder, simulator, debugger.

7
00:01:27,400 --> 00:01:35,400
When you create a project the template asks for several things. Most you can accept. Two you
should not rush.

8
00:01:35,400 --> 00:01:53,400
The first is the bundle identifier. That's your app's unique identity on the App Store, and
no two apps can share one. If somebody else registers yours, you cannot simply take it — it
can only be transferred by agreement between accounts. Pick it deliberately.

9
00:01:53,400 --> 00:01:59,000
The second is the interface: choose Storyboard, not SwiftUI, because that's what selects
UIKit.

10
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:12,200
Xcode then generates a handful of files. AppDelegate, SceneDelegate, a storyboard, and a
view controller. Don't delete any of them. AppDelegate in particular is the first thing that
runs when your app opens.

11
00:02:12,200 --> 00:02:25,000
The language. Swift has two ways to declare a value, and the distinction matters. 'var' is a
variable — it can change. 'let' is a constant — once it's set, that's it.

12
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:39,000
Reach for 'let' by default and only use 'var' when you need to change the value. It isn't
pedantry: a constant is a promise to everyone reading your code that this thing will not
move.

13
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:56,600
The core types: Int for whole numbers, Double for decimals, String for text, Bool for true
or false. Then two collection types you'll use constantly — Array, an ordered list you reach
into by index, and Dictionary, key-value pairs you reach into by key.

14
00:02:56,600 --> 00:03:07,000
Control flow is conventional. if-else, switch, for loops, while loops. One small difference
if you're coming from another language: Swift doesn't need parentheses around the condition.

15
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:20,600
Functions use the 'func' keyword, a name, typed parameters and an optional return type. One
naming convention marks your code as idiomatic: camelCase. First word lowercase, every
following word capitalised. Not snake_case with underscores.

16
00:03:20,600 --> 00:03:24,200
The single most important idea in this module: Optionals.

17
00:03:24,200 --> 00:03:37,400
Look at this model from the real app. Three of its properties have a question mark after the
type. That question mark means: this might hold a value, or it might be empty.

18
00:03:37,400 --> 00:03:57,400
Why does that exist? Because most crashes in a mobile app come from data. You expect a
field, the data doesn't have it, and the app dies in someone's hand. Optionals force you to
confront that possibility at the moment you write the code, rather than discovering it in

19
00:03:57,400 --> 00:04:11,000
You have safe ways to open an optional. 'if let' unwraps it only when there's something
inside. The double question mark supplies a fallback value. And 'guard' exits early when the
value is missing.

20
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:23,800
There's also an exclamation mark, which force-unwraps and asserts the value is definitely
there. Use it sparingly. Every force-unwrap is a small bet that you're right, and a crash if
you're wrong.

21
00:04:23,800 --> 00:04:41,000
Two more language pieces before we finish. A struct is a value type — it gets copied. A
class is a reference type — it gets shared. The practical rule this course follows: structs
for data and models, classes for behaviour and services.

22
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:54,600
And a protocol is a contract. It says what a type must be able to do, without saying how.
That sounds abstract now; in Module six it becomes the backbone of the whole architecture.

23
00:04:54,600 --> 00:05:01,000
Last topic, and it's the practical one. How does a button on screen run your code?

24
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:19,000
There are two separate connections, and you usually need both. An outlet connects the
control as an object, so code can reach in and change it — enable it, restyle it, read its
text. An action connects the behaviour, so code runs when it's tapped.

25
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:32,600
You make both by holding Control and dragging from the control into your code. Outlets go
above viewDidLoad. Actions go below it. Mixing those two up is behind a whole family of
beginner bugs.

26
00:05:32,600 --> 00:05:50,200
Here's all of that doing real work. Every row in this list is one Movie struct. The optional
properties are the poster and the online-database ID, because not every film has either. The
list scrolls, nothing crashes, and no value is forced open anywhere.

27
00:05:50,200 --> 00:05:52,700
Let's recap the key points.

28
00:05:52,700 --> 00:05:55,200
Here is your practice exercise.

29
00:05:55,200 --> 00:06:07,200
In Module two we take these foundations and build a real interface — layout, navigation,
table views, and the architecture pattern that keeps your code from turning into a mess.
